<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yajurveda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yajur Veda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yajurveda is one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the sacred texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, made up of prose formulas and verses recited by the adhvaryu priest as he performed the actions of the Vedic sacrifice. Compiled in the Kuru realm of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> early in the 1st millennium BCE, it survives in two main branches, the Black and the White Yajurveda. It is a major source for Vedic ritual and early Indian society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yajurveda.jpg" alt="Yajurveda" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1200-1000_BCE,_Vajasneyi_samhita_sample_i,_Shukla_Yajurveda,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Yajurveda joins yajus, meaning a sacrificial prayer or formula, and veda, knowledge. Michael Witzel interprets it as a knowledge text of prose mantras used in Vedic rituals. The text is a compilation of ritual-offering formulas said by a priest while an individual performed ritual actions such as those before the yajña fire. Its core belongs to the classical Mantra period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a>. It is younger than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> and roughly contemporary with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> and the Sāmaveda, and its earliest layer borrows from verses of the Rigveda.</p><p>The text is grouped into the black, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and the white, or Shukla, Yajurveda. The black label implies an unarranged, motley collection, while the white implies a well-arranged one. In the Krishna Yajurveda each recension mixes its Brahmana commentary into the collection of mantras, while in the Shukla version the two are kept apart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The earliest layer of the Yajurveda samhita holds about 1,875 verses. The Shukla Yajurveda's collection is called the Vajasaneyi Samhita, after Vajasaneya, the patronymic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>, and it has forty chapters. Only two of its many recensions survive, the Madhyandina and the Kanva, which are nearly identical. Four recensions of the Krishna Yajurveda survive: the Taittirīya, Maitrayani, Kaṭha and Kapiṣṭhala samhitas. The Maitrayani is the oldest Yajurveda samhita to survive. The mantras are typically set in a metre and call on Vedic deities such as Savita, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Agni</a>, Prajapati and Rudra.</p><p>The middle layer includes the Satapatha Brahmana, the Brahmana of the Hundred Paths, one of the largest Brahmana texts. Its youngest layer includes six primary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>: the Brihadaranyaka, Isha, Taittiriya, Katha, Shvetashvatara and Maitri. The Katha Upanishad tells of the boy Nachiketa, who meets Yama, the deity of death, and discusses the nature of the self.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The text is a useful source on agriculture and economic and social life in the Vedic era. A verse of the White Yajurveda lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, barley, beans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">sesame</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, wheat and lentils among the crops for which the offerer prays. Two of the oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Shukla Yajurveda sections, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and Western Tibet, are dated to the 12th century CE.</p><p>Most surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> remain untranslated into Western languages. The two reliable translations are from the colonial period: A. B. Keith's of the Taittiriya Samhita and Eggeling's of the Satapatha Brahmana. In the 18th century French Jesuits published the Ezourvedam, claiming it was a translation of a recension of the Yajurveda, but it was later declared a forgery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yajurveda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shruti, “what is heard”, is revealed knowledge received by the ancient seers: the four Vedas, the Rigveda, Samaveda, <b>Yajurveda</b> and Atharvaveda, each with its Brahmanas, ritual commentaries; Aranyakas, forest treatises; and Upanishads, philosophical dialogues.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…Rigveda, by far the most archaic and closest to the Indo-Iranian heritage; the mantra texts of the Atharvaveda, Samaveda and <b>Yajurveda</b>; the Samhita prose of the Black Yajurveda; the Brahmana prose, with the oldest Upanishads; and the Sutras, the last stratum of Vedic Sanskrit before c. 500 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Kuru kings patronised Brahmin priests, who arranged the hymns of the Rigveda into collections and developed the Samaveda and <b>Yajurveda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuru-kingdom">Kuru Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
